# Fixed in part 2026-11-13, defect 2494, lane b34-signals: a Heroes function
# a signal handler reaches that builds a value, run while the thread it
# interrupts is inside the C library's allocator.
# Symptom: the program died of a heap the allocator itself had corrupted,
# 5 runs of 5 on this Mac (3 killed by SIGKILL with nothing said, 2 by SIGTRAP,
# memory given back twice) and 5 of 5 in Linux arm64 (glibc's heap aborts,
# twice the runtime's own false report of a str's block that lost its mark),
# the critic's storm of panel 246, whose handler builds a string.
# Cause: the handler entered the allocator again from inside it; each call the
# runtime makes to the allocator is now bracketed by a flag of the thread's
# own, and a handler that finds it open stops the program by name before the
# allocator is entered twice.
# Rewritten 2026-11-13, lane b35-signals (panel 247's R2 and R8): since R1 a
# Heroes function given to `signal` is refused at its first signal, before
# this guard is reached, so the handler here is the program's own C, which
# reaches the function through more frames than the thunk's walk reads, the
# ground R2 leaves to this guard; its words carry R9's one remedy. Here a
# thread of the program's own C sends SIGUSR1 to `main` without pause, for at
# most 20 seconds, while `main` builds and drops small arrays, so a signal
# lands inside the allocator early.
# Neither side formats a number: the C library's `snprintf`, which an
# f-string's `{n}` calls, holds a lock of its own on Darwin, and a handler
# taking it again on the thread that holds it is killed by that library with
# nothing said, the part the repair does not reach (12 runs of 200 on this
# Mac with the storm's strings; 0 of 50 in Linux arm64, where it takes none).
# `main` prints its one line before the sender starts, for stdio's own reason.

extern "fixedbugs-2494-a-thread-of-cs-own-sends-the-signal.h"
    function storm_install(called: (function(i32) -> ())) -> i32
    function storm_start() -> i64
    function storm_stop()

# Builds an array and drops it, as any function a handler calls that keeps a
# value does.
function on_signal(sig: i32)
    seen = [sig, sig, sig]
    _ = seen.len()

# A hundred thousand small arrays built into an array and dropped with it.
function round(r: i64) -> i64
    rows: [[i64]] @= []
    i @= 0

    while i < 100_000
        rows @ rows.push([r, i])
        i @ i + 1

    return rows.len()

function main()
    _ = storm_install(called: on_signal)
    print("starting a sender aimed at main")
    started = storm_start()
    total @= 0
    r @= 0

    while r < 50
        total @ total + round(r)
        r @ r + 1

    storm_stop()
    print("sender started ", started, ", built ", total, " rows, and no signal met the allocator")
